    <title type="sort">3</title>
    <desc>Add, make, share and find the disk images of a project.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Disk images</title>
  <p>A disk image is a file that holds the disk of a virtual machine, as
  <file>debian.qcow2</file>. The <gui>Images</gui> tab has a row for each
  image of the project: its format, the size of the disk that the machine
  sees, the space that the file takes, and which disks use it, on a private
  copy or the image itself. Its state is <gui>In use</gui> while a machine
  that reads it runs or starts.</p>
  <figure>
    <title>The Images tab</title>
    <media type="image" mime="image/png" src="figures/images.png">
      <p>The Images tab with the image debian: qcow2, a disk of 1.0 GB, the
      space that its file takes, and no disk uses it.</p>
    </media>
  </figure>
  <section id="add">
    <title>Adding an image</title>
    <p><gui>Add Image</gui> offers two ways:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Existing Image…</gui></title>
        <p>A file you have. Virtualbricks reads it with <cmd>qemu-img</cmd>
        and copies it to the shared images of the workspace, unless you choose
        <gui>Use it where it is</gui>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>New Empty Disk…</gui></title>
        <p>A new file, of the <gui>Size</gui> and <gui>Format</gui> you choose,
        qcow2 or raw, in a <gui>Folder</gui>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>The same two are in the list of images of each disk of a machine.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="shared">
    <title>The shared images</title>
    <p>Each workspace has a folder of images that its projects share,
    <file>shared_images</file>. An image there can be used by several
    projects, and by several machines of each, each machine on a private copy
    of its own.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="menu">
    <title>The menu of an image</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Details…</gui></title>
        <p>The name and the description, which you can change, then what
        <cmd>qemu-img</cmd> says of the file, each disk that uses it, and the
        other projects that use the same file.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Rename…</gui></title>
        <p>A new name, in the disks that use it too.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Show in Files</gui></title>
        <p>The file, in the file manager.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Remove…</gui></title>
        <p>The image leaves the project. The dialog says which disks lose their
        image, and offers to move the file to the trash only when it is in the
        shared images and no other project uses it.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Find the File…</gui></title>
        <p>For an image whose file is missing, as one left unset at the
        import of an archive: choose where it is now. The private copies made
        on it follow it.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
</page>
